              "Through an unbiased chemical screen for small molecules capable of restoring circular nuclear morphology in Lamin A/C-depleted and HGPS human fibroblasts without cytotoxicity, Delphine Larrieu, Sébastien Britton, Мурат Demir, Raphaël Rodriguez, and Stephen P. Jackson (Science 2014; doi:10.1126/science.1252651) identified **Remodelin**. Using click-chemistry affinity capture and mass spectrometry, they discovered that Remodelin selectively binds and inhibits the acetyl-CoA catalytic pocket of **NAT10** (N-acetyltransferase 10), a nucleolar and nuclear-periphery lysine/cytidine acetyltransferase. In laminopathic cells, NAT10 hyper-acetylates α-tubulin on cytoplasmic microtubules, locking them into rigid, hyper-stable cables that deform the SUN1-tethered nuclear envelope. Inhibiting NAT10 with Remodelin (or siRNA knockdown of NAT10 or SUN1) destabilizes this pathological microtubule anchor, immediately restoring spherical nuclear circularity in both HGPS patient fibroblasts and normal aged human fibroblasts, normalizing nucleolar architecture, clearing endogenous γH2AX DNA double-strand breaks and autophagic markers, and reducing senescence-associated β-galactosidase without altering Progerin or Lamin A levels.",
